WEBVTT

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:06.350
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department, Cal State Long Beach.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:12.850
As of now, we were assuming that we have ideal timing.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:20.530
So, we were assuming that we put a signal on the input of the flip-flop, and immediately

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:29.750
after rising edge of the clock, we have that data stored inside of the flip-flop and also

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:40.200
available on the output of the flip-flop. So for everything before this we assumed

00:00:45.130 --> 00:01:01.480
so there is no delay here. Let's call it better value on input. When we provide value on input

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:22.680
of a flip-flop and flip-flops are what registers are made of immediately after

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.840
the rising edge of the clock,

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.950
the content and output.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:42.660
So that means Q of the flip-flop is available.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.380
Now think a little bit about all the examples

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:48.300
that we did with adders and subtractors

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:53.300
and think about our add sub lab assignment that we had.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:02:02.700
that we had. And you can understand now, if you remember that, that in real life we do

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:09.500
not have this ideal timing conditions. So what we practice with adder and subtractor

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:19.380
is from going to from ideal conditions to going to conditions when we had some model

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:29.100
we modeled it some delay. For flip-flops, we have to also have to acknowledge that

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:39.920
we do not live in ideal world. Although in our problems and in our labs we will

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:48.100
be working with quote-unquote ideal conditions, we need to know at least in

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.320
In theory, what are the time constraints?

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:57.180
So for flip-flops,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:13.270
we do have three time constraints that are defined.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:18.330
All of them are going to be defined

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:22.250
with respect to active clock edge.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:25.110
And in our case, that's going to be positive clock edge.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:31.130
So let's start from the one that is probably going

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:41.050
going to be the most intuitive and that is time write up. I'm a little bit

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:48.970
exaggerating with respect to how wide this time is compared to width of clock

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:58.700
signal, clock pulse, but I'm doing that so that we can actually mark the

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:04.620
timing here and so that you can actually see something. So first timing constraint

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:16.800
parameter is called TCQ or clock to queue delay. If you look at, listen a

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:25.120
little bit for this hint that is given in the in the name, from the moment the

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:36.200
clock, positive edge of clock happens to the moment that Q is actually available, new value

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:58.020
of the Q is available. So this is time needed after a positive or active clock age for

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:20.420
the output to acquire or assume the new value. So that's the first one. Now we have a little

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:29.380
bit of a different thing and that is a parameter. So this timing parameter

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:37.580
refers to Q output. When does Q output become valid? When does Q output get

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:50.340
updated with the new new content? Now we have something that is about the input

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:58.660
signal. So the next two time constraints are defined for the input signal. And let me first

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:23.570
do a visual representation. So this time here is going to be called setup, setup time. That

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:31.010
is the minimum time that inputs need to be stable before the active positive edge of

00:06:31.010 --> 00:07:03.660
clock so that we have correct data that is stored inside. So this is minimum time that before the

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:32.000
active, if we don't respect this minimum time, incorrect data will be maybe stored. So that is

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:39.440
sub time before clock edge. And final third parameter is also refers to the input data

00:07:39.440 --> 00:08:00.900
and that is going to be called hold time. And that hold time is here, so T hold,

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:20.140
hold time, it's minimum time, it's a minimum time that input needs to be stable after the

00:08:20.140 --> 00:09:21.930
active positive edge for the correct data to be stored. It is important that we know

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:30.450
that these three times do exist as timing parameters and as constraints

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:37.410
because in consecutive classes you will be using these times in order to figure

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:45.690
out how short a clock cycle can be. Basically what is the maximum frequency

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:46.970
that you can do.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:52.870
So please pay attention that T CQ, T clock to Q delay,

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:59.610
refers to the output value and input signal

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:05.210
where setup at hold are defined for the input signal.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:07.170
Hope this was helpful.

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:08.010
Thank you.

